despite healthy BGP</label><label><input name="q11" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> STP elects one VTEP</label><button onclick="cq(11)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f11"></div><span id="r11" style="display:none">Correct answer: Inconsistency can black-hole traffic despite healthy BGP. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>12. Why run OSPF/BGP on a Junos EVPN IRB?</h3><label><input name="q12" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> To replace VXLAN</label><label><input name="q12" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> To advertise STP roots</label><label><input name="q12" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> To create MPLS automatically</label><label><input name="q12" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> To form routed adjacencies to appliances/CEs while EVPN carries the overlay</label><button onclick="cq(12)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f12"></div><span id="r12" style="display:none">Correct answer: To form routed adjacencies to appliances/CEs while EVPN carries the overlay. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>13. Arista Type-5 DCI gateway resembles what?</h3><label><input name="q13" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> A redistribution boundary that must preserve provenance</label><label><input name="q13" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> A pure L2 hub</label><label><input name="q13" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> DHCP relay</label><label><input name="q13" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> STP root</label><button onclick="cq(13)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f13"></div><span id="r13" style="display:none">Correct answer: A redistribution boundary that must preserve provenance. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>14. Why can PAN-OS OSPF migration change policy behavior?</h3><label><input name="q14" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> OSPF is removed</label><label><input name="q14" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Legacy path-type/area matches do not map one-for-one to ARE redistribution</label><label><input name="q14" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> BGP becomes mandatory</label><label><input name="q14" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> VLAN IDs change</label><button onclick="cq(14)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f14"></div><span id="r14" style="display:none">Correct answer: Legacy path-type/area matches do not map one-for-one to ARE redistribution. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>15. FRR IS-IS SR design caution?</h3><label><input name="q15" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> FRR cannot run IS-IS</label><label><input name="q15" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> SR requires VXLAN</label><label><input name="q15" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Verify optional feature support/limitations across vendors</label><label><input name="q15" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Only RIP advertises Prefix-SIDs</label><button onclick="cq(15)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f15"></div><span id="r15" style="display:none">Correct answer: Verify optional feature support/limitations across vendors. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>16. Why advertise Type-5 subnet routes instead of every host route?</h3><label><input name="q16" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Disable routing</label><label><input name="q16" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Make VXLAN L2-only</label><label><input name="q16" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Force all traffic through core</label><label><input name="q16" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Reduce control-plane state and improve scale</label><button onclick="cq(16)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f16"></div><span id="r16" style="display:none">Correct answer: Reduce control-plane state and improve scale. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>17. What does EOS control/data-plane separation teach?</h3><label><input name="q17" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Route presence does not prove hardware forwarding</label><label><input name="q17" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Control plane forwards all packets</label><label><input name="q17" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> There is no FIB</label><label><input name="q17" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Static routes bypass routing</label><button onclick="cq(17)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f17"></div><span id="r17" style="display:none">Correct answer: Route presence does not prove hardware forwarding. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>18. EVPN route exists but VTEP next hop unresolved. First fault boundary?</h3><label><input name="q18" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> RT import</label><label><input name="q18" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Underlay recursive reachability</label><label><input name="q18" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> STP</label><label><input name="q18" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> BGP TCP only</label><button onclick="cq(18)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f18"></div><span id="r18" style="display:none">Correct answer: Underlay recursive reachability. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>19. FortiGate EVPN reachability correct but CPU high: check what?</h3><label><input name="q19" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> OSPF priority</label><label><input name="q19" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> BGP cluster ID</label><label><input name="q19" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> NP7/VXLAN offload behavior</label><label><input name="q19" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Native VLAN only</label><button onclick="cq(19)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f19"></div><span id="r19" style="display:none">Correct answer: NP7/VXLAN offload behavior. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>20. When porting route policy between vendors, translate what first?</h3><label><input name="q20" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Exact command spelling</label><label><input name="q20" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Interface names</label><label><input name="q20" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Router ID</label><label><input name="q20" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Business intent and attachment stage</label><button onclick="cq(20)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f20"></div><span id="r20" style="display:none">Correct answer: Business intent and attachment stage. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>21. BGP best path has unreachable NEXT_HOP. Correct fix?</h3><label><input name="q21" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Make next hop reachable or use next-hop-self correctly</label><label><input name="q21" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Raise MED</label><label><input name="q21" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Disable recursion</label><label><input name="q21" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Redistribute to RIP</label><button onclick="cq(21)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f21"></div><span id="r21" style="display:none">Correct answer: Make next hop reachable or use next-hop-self correctly. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>22. OSPF Area 0 is what?</h3><label><input name="q22" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> NSSA only</label><label><input name="q22" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Backbone for inter-area connectivity</label><label><input name="q22" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> BGP RR cluster</label><label><input name="q22" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> VXLAN VNI</label><button onclick="cq(22)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f22"></div><span id="r22" style="display:none">Correct answer: Backbone for inter-area connectivity. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>23. OSPF Type-5 LSA carries what?</h3><label><input name="q23" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Router-LSA only</label><label><input name="q23" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> EVPN IP prefix</label><label><input name="q23" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> AS-external reachability</label><label><input name="q23" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> BGP community</label><button onclick="cq(23)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f23"></div><span id="r23" style="display:none">Correct answer: AS-external reachability. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>24. E1 versus E2 key difference?</h3><label><input name="q24" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> E1 IPv6-only</label><label><input name="q24" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> E2 NSSA-only</label><label><input name="q24" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> E1 cannot redistribute</label><label><input name="q24" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> E1 includes internal cost toward ASBR; E2 generally uses external metric as dominant</label><button onclick="cq(24)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f24"></div><span id="r24" style="display:none">Correct answer: E1 includes internal cost toward ASBR; E2 generally uses external metric as dominant. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>25. Why does EIGRP redistribution need a seed metric?</h3><label><input name="q25" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> To build a usable composite metric</label><label><input name="q25" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> BGP requires it</label><label><input name="q25" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> STP uses it</label><label><input name="q25" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> VXLAN uses it</label><button onclick="cq(25)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f25"></div><span id="r25" style="display:none">Correct answer: To build a usable composite metric. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>26. EIGRP loop-free alternate condition?</h3><label><input name="q26" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> STP root guard</label><label><input name="q26" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> DUAL feasibility condition</label><label><input name="q26" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> BGP MED</label><label><input name="q26" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> EVPN RT import</label><button onclick="cq(26)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f26"></div><span id="r26" style="display:none">Correct answer: DUAL feasibility condition. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Historical</span><h3>27. RIP maximum usable hop count?</h3><label><input name="q27" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> 16</label><label><input name="q27" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> 255</label><label><input name="q27" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> 15</label><label><input name="q27" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> 8</label><button onclick="cq(27)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f27"></div><span id="r27" style="display:none">Correct answer: 15. The distractors act at a differen